JUnit



Lifecycle of a Junit5 test case:



Lifecycle.PER_METHOD(default)

Test Class instance is created for each Test Method.





Lifecycle.PER_CLASS

Test Class instance is created only once.









@BeforeAll: runs once before all tests, usually for expensive setup that can be shared across 

all test cases like DB connection etc. 



@BeforeEach: Runs before each test case, usually for setting fresh test data like 

creating new objects, adding some temp data in DB for testing etc. 



@Test: actual test method 



@AfterEach: Runs after each test case, to clean up the resource like close file, deleting temp data etc. 



@AfterAll: runs once after all tests, clean up resources like closing DB connection etc. 





Why Lifecycle.PER_METHOD is preferred, because: 

It provides test isolation. So they can run in any order. 

Easy debugging when any test case fails. 

Safe for parallel execution. 



Go for Lifecycle.PER_CLASS only when: 

You want to shared state across test cases. 

Your test code is thread safe. 





NOTE : Multiple @BeforeAll / @BeforeEach / @AfterEach / @AfterAll can be used But:Order is not guaranteed between execution of these 2 methods.









